//
// A destructuring with more names than the tuple has elements is REFUSED by name, not
// with an internal compiler error (loft#959).
//
// The arity check was already there and already an `Error` — but it only REPORTED, and
// lowering carried straight on to index the tuple with an LHS position it does not have.
//
// A scalar tuple survived that: it is at most 8 bytes, so `ref_def_nr == u32::MAX` and
// the element read is `TupleGet(tmp, i)`, which indexes nothing.  Widen the tuple past
// 8 bytes — ONE `vector<T>` element is enough — and the return becomes the synthetic
// `__tuple<…>` struct, where `rhs_elems[i]` and the per-attribute `offs[i]` are real
// indexes.  `(a, b, c, d, e) = <4-tuple>` then panicked with "the len is 4 but the index
// is 4", and the author got an ICE where the scalar spelling prints a diagnostic.
//
// ⚠ THE TUPLE MUST CONTAIN A COLLECTION.  An all-scalar version of this file passes with
// the fix reverted — it never reaches the indexing path — so it would guard nothing.
// That is also why the issue was filed as "scalar tuples report cleanly": the reporter
// had found the boundary, not a difference in the check.
//
// The report reached this through a local `fn` shadowing a library `pub fn` of the same
// name and a different arity, and noted the redefinition was never reported.  It was
// being detected all along; the ICE discarded the diagnostics collected before it.  With
// the panic gone that shape prints both errors — `Cannot redefine` and this arity one —
// which is exactly what the scalar spelling always printed.  No library is needed to
// pin the ICE itself, so this file does it in one program.

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Tuple arity mismatch: left has 5 names, right has 4 elements

fn wide(n: integer) -> (vector<single>, vector<single>, vector<single>, single) {
  z: vector<single> = [for _ in 0..n { 1.0f }];
  (z, z, z, 2.0f)
}

fn main() {
  (a, b, c, d, e) = wide(3);
  print("{len(a)}{len(b)}{len(c)}{d}{e}\n");
}
